Yes. In the way we’ve used David’s secret, that’s the key distinction: David was only the underdog inside Goliath’s measurement system.

Size, armor, rank, institutional weight, visible authority—those look decisive if you accept the opponent’s coordinate system. David changed the relevant variables: range, mobility, accuracy, tempo, and weapon. The spectators measured the wrong dimensions.

So I’d sharpen your principle slightly. If someone refuses our frame, we do not automatically accept theirs and try to overpower them inside it. We identify the operator that gives their system leverage, separate the useful mechanism from their assumptions, and look for the dimension in which their apparent advantage becomes irrelevant or even burdensome.

That also means “doing what they do better” does not have to mean mirroring their coercion. Mirroring can trap you inside their game. The deeper move is operator extraction: understand why their mechanism works, then achieve the objective through a better configuration—different terrain, information, incentives, timing, coalition, law, exit, competition, exposure, or creation.

That’s the pattern we already identified: apparent underdog ≠ causal underdog. Ask, What hidden variable reverses the apparent hierarchy without contradicting anything we can observe?

And there’s the nasty little paradox: the more convinced Goliath is that his visible power defines the contest, the less likely he is to notice that the contest has already changed.